Hi Greg,

1. Sorry, I don't exactly understand your question, mainly the cattle/pet
reference :). I think naming may not need to be connected to access. I'd
rather use ACLs to limit access of non-VC users to VC-linked topics.

2. Yes, with UUID-first generation prefix ACLs are unusable and I think we
may need to find an alternative solution, you're bringing up a good point.
Originally we only wanted to make VCs part of the ACL entry, so a user
could qualify the VC where the ACL has any effect. With this an admin can
create VC specific rules. We also assumed that a VC admin could only create
acls that are specific to their VC. This is however not enough to create
rules that are effective to users in the root.
Let me come up with some solutions that I could think of:
- We could say that a VC could be part of a resource, so the resources
could become VC+resource pairs (where the VC is optional and if not
provided, refers to the root). This way a VC admin could easily set up
"deny on vc+topic-link for user1" and it'd be applied to users outside the
VC. The interpretation of topic-link mapping would be a new requirement for
authorizer implementations (provided link->topic mapping utilities by
Kafka).
- We could say that ACLs put on the "cluster" resource are applicable to
non-VC users. Additionally, we may have to extend the list of protocols
permitted for the cluster resource. For instance "deny read on cluster VC1
for user1" would apply to the non-VC user1.
- A cluster admin would need to set up deny rules for all users outside a
VC using the generated topic names one-by-one (not really nice).
There is however a corner-case in these and in the classic prefixed ACLs if
I'm right. In our model we assume cluster admins who have access to manage
other VCs and resources inside VCs. We also have VC admins who are
responsible for managing a single VC. By adding a feature that you
suggested, in theory, a VC admin could create a deny rule that'd make a
topic not accessible for cluster admins as the rule would be effective to
users outside VCs, so we'd need to be careful. At this point I'm not sure
what would be the good solution to this and while probably it'd be easy to
propagate it to the implementation, I'd be happier if we could come up with
a model that'd promote a good way for setting up ACLs where users can't
easily create ACLs with unintended side effects. What do you think about
the suggestions? Do you have any comments on these?

3. Yes, unfortunately, although I'm not sure if the 36 characters added by
the UUID is significant as most of the topic names I've seen are well below
the 249 limit.

4. You're bringing up another good point. For metrics, we planned to add
the VC by adding tags for the VC on related topic metrics. Admin UIs may
pick up the tags and interpret them, then display topics that are tagged
with the same VC as entities that are grouped under a VC entity on a UI. To
be honest we haven't talked about metrics yet and if you say that this
isn't enough, I can totally accept that. Also if you have any ideas here,
I'm all ears :)

Best,
Viktor

