Dear Community,

I am truly delighted to see the KIP-1150 proposal put forth by Josep, which=20
presents an exciting new architecture for the cloud economy.

It was an honor to hear Ziming and Stanislav mention AutoMQ[1] during the=20
discussion. Over the past few years, AutoMQ has dedicated itself to this=20
direction, creating what we call a "Stateless Kafka" that is entirely built o=
n=20
object storage. AutoMQ has quickly gained recognition from numerous medium to=
=20
large enterprises. For instance, Grab, based in Singapore, has already put it=
=20
into use. We even have single clients whose peak throughput has exceeded=20
50GiB/s.

As a production-ready solution, the core storage layer of AutoMQ can be simpl=
y=20
understood as a new implementation of Kafka's LogSegment. From a feasibility =

standpoint, it could serve as another storage engine for the community,=20
developing in parallel with the existing ISR-based storage engine. If it=20
aligns with the community's roadmap, we would be more than willing to discuss=
=20
the possibility of merging AutoMQ's code into Kafka. This is a new option for=
=20
everyone to consider.

If this is not the appropriate thread for discussion, we are open to starting=
=20
a new thread for a more comprehensive discussion.

Thanks again for KIP-1150 and everyone involved in this discussion.

[1] https://github.com/AutoMQ/automq

Best Regards, =20
Xiaorui =20
Co-founder of AutoMQ

On 2025/04/20 20:04:14 Stanislav Kozlovski wrote:
> This is an amazing initiative. Huge kudos for driving it. We should incorpo=
rate it one way or another.
>=20
> I have a suggestion I'd like to hear your thoughts on. I'm cognizant of the=
 effort required for KIP-1150 so I don't necessarily want to increase the sco=
pe - but thinking about this early on can help design later on, plus shape th=
e motivation.
>=20
> The idea is to introduce support for replicationless acks=3D1 writes. This =
would be very similar to how AutoMQ's WAL+S3 feature works, as far as I under=
stand it.
>=20
> Could we have Diskless Brokers serve acks=3D1 produce requests by immediate=
ly persisting the data on disk (not sure if we should use fsync or not), resp=
onding to the request, and then still asynchronously batching said data with =
regular acks=3Dall data via the "diskless.append.commit.interval.ms"/ "diskle=
ss.append.buffer.max.bytes" configs?
>=20
> If I'm not mistaken, this would offer very similar guarantees as today's ac=
ks=3D1 requests, where a period of low durability exists b/w the time the lea=
der persists to its local disk and the time all followers persist to their di=
sk. Granted, in traditional Kafka this period is probably no more than a hund=
red milliseconds, and here it'd be at least 2x higher. But I believe that giv=
en the major savings, many acks=3D1 users will be happy to make the tradeoff.
>=20
> While on the topic of cost, I hastily ran some cost calculations and found =
that the KIP should reduce replication costs by more than 80x. (https://topic=
partition.io/blog/kip-1150-diskless-topics-in-apache-kafka). There may be som=
e errors there as the batch coordinator RPC and merging isn't fully fleshed o=
ut - but I believe it's directionally correct. It may be worth to add that to=
 the motivation in one way or another - so as to be able to quantify the numb=
ers.
>=20
> Best,
> Stanislav
>=20
